<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Firth of Forth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Firth of Forth is the estuary of the River Forth on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> on its southern shore and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fife">Fife</a> to the north. In Roman and early medieval times it marked a frontier zone between the Votadini and Gododdin to the south and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> to the north. Northumbrian power reached it in the 7th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth.jpg" alt="Firth of Forth" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Nugent · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Forth_bridges_from_the_air_(geograph_5835049).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
